+# Overdue fines and member accounts
+
+## Why
+
+Late returns currently cost nothing, so DVDs come back weeks late. Members now
+get an account: a late return charges an overdue fine, staff can add manual
+charges, members can pay, and members who owe too much cannot borrow until
+they pay it down.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+All on `LendingService` in `src/lending/service.py`:
+
+- `return_copy(copy_id)` keeps its signature and return value; a late return now also charges the fine.
+- `balance(member_id: str) -> Decimal` — what the member owes right now.
+- `add_charge(member_id: str, amount: Decimal, reason: str) -> Decimal` — manual staff charge; returns the new balance.
+- `pay(member_id: str, amount: Decimal) -> Decimal` — records a payment; returns the new balance.
+
+Add to `src/lending/errors.py`:
+
+- `AccountBlocked(LendingError)`, raised by `checkout` when the member owes too much.
+- `InvalidAmount(LendingError)` for amounts that can't be accepted.
+
+Unknown members keep raising `MemberNotFound`.
+
+## Rules
+
+- **Rates**: 0.25 per late day for books, 1.00 per late day for DVDs.
+- **Children are never fined.** A child's late return is processed normally and charges nothing.
+- **Late days** run from the day after `due_on` up to and including the
+  return day, not counting Sundays (the library is closed, so nobody could
+  have returned the copy). There is no grace period.
+- **Cap**: one loan's fine never exceeds its title's replacement cost.
+- **When the fine is charged**: only when the copy comes back. Copies still out and overdue add
+  nothing to the balance. Each late return adds its own fine, and fines add up.
+- **Block**: a member whose balance is 10.00 or more cannot check out. The
+  threshold is the same for every category. The block only affects checkout;
+  returns, payments and charges always work.
+- **Valid amounts** (for `add_charge` and `pay`): a `Decimal` that is positive, finite and a whole
+  number of cents. Whole cents is judged by value, so `Decimal("1.50")` and
+  `Decimal("1.5")` are both accepted. Floats, ints and other types are refused.
+- **No credit**: a payment larger than the current balance is refused.
+- **Manual charges** need a non-empty reason. They go on the same balance as fines and count toward the block.
+- **A refused operation changes nothing.** A refused checkout creates no loan and leaves
+  the copy on the shelf. A refused payment or charge leaves the balance unchanged.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. A newly registered member's `balance` is 0.
+2. A book returned on its due date charges nothing, and so does any earlier return.
+3. A book returned one day after its due date (not a Sunday) charges 0.25.
+4. A book due Mon 23 March 2026 and returned Mon 30 March 2026 charges 1.50 (6 late days, because Sunday 29 March doesn't count).
+5. A DVD returned 3 days late (Mon–Wed, no Sunday) charges 3.00.
+6. A loan due on a Saturday and returned on the following Monday has 1 late day.
+7. A child returning a DVD weeks late is charged nothing. The loan closes and the copy goes back on the shelf.
+8. A fine that would exceed the title's replacement cost is charged at exactly the replacement cost.
+9. Fines from two late returns add up in `balance`.
+10. A copy that is overdue but not yet returned adds nothing to `balance`.
+11. `return_copy` returns the same closed `Loan` it did before, with `returned_on` set.
+12. `add_charge` adds the amount to the balance and returns the new balance.
+13. `pay` lowers the balance by the amount and returns the new balance. Paying the full balance brings it to 0.
+14. `checkout` raises `AccountBlocked` when the balance is exactly 10.00, and also when it is above 10.00.
+15. `checkout` succeeds when the balance is 9.99.
+16. After a blocked member pays their balance below 10.00, `checkout` succeeds again.
+17. Manual charges count toward the block.
+18. A blocked checkout creates no loan: `open_loans` is unchanged and the copy stays `AVAILABLE`.
+19. A blocked member can still return copies, pay, and receive charges. A late return may push them over the threshold.
+20. `pay` and `add_charge` raise `InvalidAmount` for zero, negative, fractional-cent, non-finite, float or int amounts, and leave the balance unchanged.
+21. `add_charge` raises `InvalidAmount` for an empty or whitespace-only reason, and leaves the balance unchanged.
+22. `pay` raises `InvalidAmount` for more than the current balance, including any payment at all when the balance is 0, and leaves the balance unchanged.
+23. `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay` raise `MemberNotFound` for an unknown member. `checkout` still does too.
+24. `AccountBlocked` and `InvalidAmount` are subclasses of `LendingError`.
+25. All existing tests still pass.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Waiving or refunding fines.
+- Charges for lost or damaged items.
+- Credit balances or prepayment.
+- A public API for transaction history. Charges and payments are kept in an internal ledger, but it isn't exposed.
+- Counting unreturned overdue items toward the balance or the block.
+- Changes to `overdue_report` or to loan periods and limits.
+- Holidays or any closures other than Sundays.
+
+---
+
+# Holds
+
+## Why
+
+When every copy of a title is out, members can only keep asking at the desk.
+With holds, a member joins a queue for the title. A returned copy is set aside
+for the first person in line, who gets a few days to collect it. A daily job
+clears holds nobody collected and charges a no-show fee, so set-aside copies
+don't sit on the hold shelf forever.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+On `LendingService` in `src/lending/service.py`:
+
+- `place_hold(member_id: str, isbn: str) -> Hold`
+- `cancel_hold(hold_id: str) -> Hold`
+- `expire_holds() -> list[Hold]` — the daily job; returns the holds it expired.
+- `holds_for(isbn: str) -> list[Hold]` — active holds on the title, first in line first.
+- `return_copy` and `checkout` keep their signatures.
+
+In `src/lending/models.py`:
+
+- dataclass `Hold(hold_id: str, member_id: str, isbn: str, placed_on: date, status: HoldStatus, copy_id: str | None, pickup_by: date | None)`
+- enum `HoldStatus` with `WAITING, READY, FULFILLED, CANCELLED, EXPIRED`
+- new member `CopyStatus.ON_HOLD_SHELF`
+
+In `src/lending/errors.py`:
+
+- `HoldNotAllowed(LendingError)`
+- `HoldNotFound(LendingError)`
+
+Existing error classes are used where they already fit: `MemberNotFound`,
+`TitleNotFound`, `AccountBlocked`, `CopyNotAvailable`.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Active hold**: a hold that is `WAITING` or `READY`.
+- **WAITING**: in the queue, with no copy set aside yet (`copy_id` and `pickup_by` are `None`).
+- **READY**: a copy is set aside on the hold shelf for this member (`copy_id` is
+  that copy, and `pickup_by` is the last day they can collect it).
+- **Copy in circulation**: any copy that is not `WITHDRAWN`.
+- **Pickup window**: the day the copy is set aside + 7 days. If that is a Sunday, it moves to the Monday.
+- **Line order**: the order the holds were placed. Holds placed on the same day keep that order too.
+
+## Rules
+
+- **Placing a hold** is refused with `HoldNotAllowed` when:
+  - a copy of the title is `AVAILABLE` on the shelf. Copies set aside for
+    someone else (`ON_HOLD_SHELF`) count as out;
+  - the title has no copies in circulation (none added, or all withdrawn);
+  - the member already has an active hold on the title;
+  - the member has a copy of the title on loan right now.
+- **Hold block**: a member whose balance is 5.00 or more can't place a hold;
+  this is refused with `AccountBlocked`. It's deliberately lower than the 10.00
+  borrowing bar, because a hold ties up a copy for someone who already owes the library.
+- Unknown member → `MemberNotFound`. Unknown ISBN → `TitleNotFound` (for both
+  `place_hold` and `holds_for`).
+- **Returning a copy** whose title has WAITING holds sets the copy aside
+  (`ON_HOLD_SHELF`) for the first WAITING hold in line, which becomes READY with
+  a pickup window starting on the return day. With nobody waiting, the copy goes
+  back on the shelf as before. `return_copy` returns the closed `Loan` as before,
+  and fines are charged as before.
+- **Collecting**: the holder collects with an ordinary `checkout` of the
+  set-aside copy, and the hold becomes FULFILLED. Anyone else checking out that
+  copy gets `CopyNotAvailable`. The holder's checkout follows the normal rules (loan
+  limit, 10.00 block). If it's refused, nothing changes: the hold stays READY,
+  the copy stays on the hold shelf, and the pickup window keeps running.
+- **Checking out another copy of a held title**: if a member with an active
+  hold on a title checks out a different copy of that title, the hold becomes
+  FULFILLED. If it was READY, its set-aside copy moves on (see *Moving a copy on*).
+- **Cancelling**: `cancel_hold` works on WAITING and READY holds and never
+  charges anything. Cancelling a READY hold moves its copy on. Cancelling a hold
+  that isn't active → `HoldNotAllowed`. Unknown id → `HoldNotFound`.
+- **Expiry** (`expire_holds`): every READY hold whose `pickup_by` is before
+  today becomes EXPIRED (the pickup day itself is still in time). Its copy
+  moves on, and the member is charged a 1.00 no-show fee. The fee goes on
+  the same balance as fines and counts toward both the 10.00 borrowing block and
+  the 5.00 hold block. Children never pay it. WAITING holds never expire.
+- **Moving a copy on** (after a cancelled, expired or bypassed READY hold): the
+  copy is set aside for the next WAITING hold in line, which becomes READY with a
+  pickup window starting today. If nobody is waiting, the copy goes back on the shelf (`AVAILABLE`).
+- **A refused operation changes nothing.**
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `place_hold` on a title whose every copy is on loan returns a `Hold` that is
+   WAITING, has `placed_on` = today and `copy_id`/`pickup_by` `None`, and it appears in `holds_for`.
+2. `place_hold` raises `HoldNotAllowed` when any copy of the title is `AVAILABLE`.
+3. `place_hold` succeeds when the only copies not on loan are `ON_HOLD_SHELF` for someone else.
+4. `place_hold` raises `HoldNotAllowed` for a title with no copies, and for one whose copies are all withdrawn.
+5. `place_hold` raises `HoldNotAllowed` when the member already has a WAITING
+   hold on the title, and also when they have a READY one.
+6. `place_hold` raises `HoldNotAllowed` when the member has a copy of that title on loan.
+7. A member can hold again after their previous hold on the title was cancelled, expired or fulfilled.
+8. `place_hold` raises `AccountBlocked` when the member's balance is exactly
+   5.00, and when it's above 5.00. It succeeds at 4.99.
+9. `place_hold` raises `MemberNotFound` / `TitleNotFound` for an unknown member / ISBN.
+10. A refused `place_hold` adds no hold: `holds_for` is unchanged.
+11. Children can place holds.
+12. `holds_for` lists only WAITING and READY holds, in the order they were
+    placed. Cancelled, expired and fulfilled holds are left out. `holds_for` on a title with no holds is `[]`.
+13. Returning a copy of a title with WAITING holds puts the copy `ON_HOLD_SHELF`.
+    The first hold in line becomes READY, with `copy_id` = that copy and
+    `pickup_by` = return day + 7. The other holds stay WAITING.
+14. When return day + 7 is a Sunday, `pickup_by` is the following Monday.
+15. `return_copy` still returns the closed `Loan` and still charges any late fine when the copy goes to a hold.
+16. Returning a copy with nobody waiting puts it back `AVAILABLE`, as before.
+17. The holder checking out their set-aside copy gets a normal loan. The hold
+    becomes FULFILLED and leaves `holds_for`, and the copy is `ON_LOAN`.
+18. Anyone other than the holder checking out a set-aside copy gets `CopyNotAvailable`, and nothing changes.
+19. If the holder's checkout is refused (`AccountBlocked` or `LoanLimitReached`),
+    the hold stays READY with the same `pickup_by` and the copy stays `ON_HOLD_SHELF`.
+20. A member with a WAITING hold who checks out another copy of the same title has that hold FULFILLED.
+21. A member with a READY hold who checks out a different copy of the same title
+    has that hold FULFILLED. The set-aside copy moves on to the next in line, or
+    back to the shelf if nobody is waiting.
+22. `cancel_hold` on a WAITING hold returns it CANCELLED and it leaves `holds_for`.
+23. `cancel_hold` on a READY hold returns it CANCELLED. Its copy goes to the next
+    WAITING hold (READY, `pickup_by` = today + 7, Monday if Sunday), or back to
+    `AVAILABLE` if nobody is waiting. No fee is charged.
+24. `cancel_hold` on a FULFILLED, CANCELLED or EXPIRED hold raises `HoldNotAllowed`.
+    On an unknown id it raises `HoldNotFound`.
+25. `expire_holds` leaves a READY hold alone on its `pickup_by` day and expires it the day after.
+26. An expired hold's member is charged 1.00 (adults only; a child is charged nothing).
+27. An expired hold's copy goes to the next WAITING hold (READY, `pickup_by` =
+    today + 7, Monday if Sunday), or back to `AVAILABLE` if nobody is waiting.
+28. `expire_holds` returns exactly the holds it expired, each with status
+    EXPIRED. Running it again the same day returns `[]` and charges nothing more.
+29. WAITING holds are never expired, however long they wait.
+30. The no-show fee counts toward the borrowing block (10.00) and the hold block (5.00).
+31. `HoldNotAllowed` and `HoldNotFound` are subclasses of `LendingError`.
+32. All existing tests still pass.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Routing newly added copies (`add_copy`) to the queue: they go on the shelf as `AVAILABLE` even if people are waiting.
+- A cap on how many holds a member may have.
+- Notifying members (email, SMS) that a copy is ready.
+- Holds on a specific copy, or choosing a pickup branch.
+- Withdrawing a copy from the hold shelf (`withdraw_copy` still refuses anything not `AVAILABLE`).
+- Changes to fines, loan periods, loan limits or `overdue_report`.
+- Renewals (see `ROADMAP.md`).
+
+---
+
+# Renewals
+
+## Why
+
+Members ask to keep books longer. Today staff return the copy and check it out
+again, which closes one loan and opens a new one, so the loan history no longer
+shows one continuous borrowing. A renewal extends the open loan in place. It
+also must not become a way to escape fines, jump the queue, or keep books
+forever.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan` in `src/lending/service.py` — renews the open loan in
+  place (same `loan_id`) and returns it.
+- `Loan` in `src/lending/models.py` gets a field `renewals: int` (default `0`): how many times the
+  loan has been renewed.
+- Add `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` to `src/lending/errors.py` for renewals that can't be granted;
+  unknown loan ids keep raising `LoanNotFound`, and keep using the other existing error classes where
+  they already fit.
+
+`return_copy`, `checkout` and every other existing method keep their signatures.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Renewal day**: today, by the service's clock.
+- **Renewal window**: from 5 calendar days before the loan's current `due_on`
+  onwards, with no upper end. A loan due Mon 23 March 2026 can be renewed from
+  Wed 18 March 2026, and also after 23 March (overdue).
+- **Accrued fine**: the overdue fine the loan would be charged if the copy were
+  returned on the renewal day, using the same rates, Sunday rule, cap and child
+  exemption as a return.
+
+## Rules
+
+- **Same loan**: a renewal keeps the same `Loan` (same `loan_id`, `copy_id`,
+  `member_id` and `borrowed_on`). It stays open and the copy stays `ON_LOAN`.
+  `renewals` goes up by 1. `loan_history` still shows one loan.
+- **New due date**: the renewal day + the normal loan period, the same as a
+  checkout made that day would get (21 days for a book; if that lands on a
+  Sunday, the Monday).
+- **Refused with `RenewalRefused`** when:
+  - the loan is already returned;
+  - the copy is a DVD (only books can be renewed);
+  - the loan has already been renewed 2 times;
+  - the renewal day is before the renewal window;
+  - the member's balance is above 0.00 when they ask (*paid up only*);
+  - anyone is WAITING in the queue for the loan's title. READY holds don't count.
+- Unknown `loan_id` → `LoanNotFound`.
+- **Not applied**: the borrowing limit (a member at their limit can still renew)
+  and the 10.00 borrowing block (paid-up-only is stricter and replaces it).
+- **Overdue renewal**: renewing an overdue loan charges its accrued fine to the
+  member's account as part of the renewal. Lateness is then counted against the
+  new due date. Lateness before a renewal never just disappears: it is charged
+  at the renewal or, if the renewal is refused, when the copy comes back.
+- **Cap across renewals**: the total fines for one loan, counting what's charged
+  at each renewal and at the final return, never go above the title's
+  replacement cost.
+- **Children** follow the same rules. They are never fined, so an overdue
+  renewal charges them nothing.
+- **A refused renewal changes nothing**: no fine is charged, and `due_on`,
+  `renewals`, the copy and the holds all stay as they were.
+- When several refusals apply, which one is raised isn't specified.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `Loan.renewals` is 0 for a new checkout.
+2. Renewing a book loan inside the window returns the same `Loan` object
+   (same `loan_id`, `copy_id`, `member_id`, `borrowed_on`), still open, with
+   `renewals` = 1 and `due_on` = renewal day + 21 days. `get_loan(loan_id)`
+   returns it too.
+3. After a renewal the copy is still `ON_LOAN`, `open_loans` still has one entry for it,
+   and `loan_history` has no extra loan.
+4. When renewal day + 21 is a Sunday, the new `due_on` is the following Monday.
+5. A loan due Mon 23 March 2026 can be renewed on Wed 18 March 2026 but not on
+   Tue 17 March 2026 (`RenewalRefused`). Renewing on the borrow day is refused.
+6. A loan can be renewed twice; the third `renew` raises `RenewalRefused` and leaves
+   `renewals` at 2.
+7. Renewing a DVD loan raises `RenewalRefused`.
+8. Renewing a returned loan raises `RenewalRefused`. An unknown loan id raises `LoanNotFound`.
+9. A member whose balance is 0.01 or more can't renew (`RenewalRefused`). After paying it
+   off, they can.
+10. A WAITING hold on the title by anyone blocks renewal (`RenewalRefused`). A READY
+    hold with nobody WAITING doesn't block it. Once the queue has no WAITING holds left, renewal works.
+11. A member at their loan limit can renew.
+12. Renewing an overdue book charges the accrued fine (e.g. 3 open days late → 0.75)
+    and still sets `due_on` = renewal day + 21.
+13. Renewing on or before the due date charges nothing.
+14. After an overdue renewal, returning the copy on or before the new due date charges
+    nothing more. Returning it late charges only for days past the new due date.
+15. A child renewing an overdue book is charged nothing.
+16. Fines on one loan, at renewal and at return together, never exceed the title's
+    replacement cost. If the renewal already charged the full replacement cost, the late return charges nothing.
+17. A refused renewal changes nothing: same `due_on`, same `renewals`, same balance,
+    copy still `ON_LOAN`. This holds even when the loan is overdue. Its lateness is
+    still charged in full when the copy is returned.
+18. `RenewalRefused` is a subclass of `LendingError`.
+19. All existing tests still pass.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Renewing DVDs or other non-book material.
+- Self-service or online renewal channels, and reminders/notifications.
+- Recording each renewal's date or the original due date on the loan (only the count).
+- Changes to loan periods, loan limits, fine rates, holds, or `overdue_report`
+  (which naturally reports against the current `due_on`).
+- Staff overrides of any refusal.
